course: dictionary
lesson: 1392
title: Meet the capybara
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A capybara is an animal, and its name is a word you may not have met before. You can work out much of what it means from the words around it. «The Pantanal in Brazil is the world's biggest tropical wetland, where jaguars hunt caimans and capybaras (giant rodents).» The brackets tell you that a capybara is a giant rodent.
explain: Read the clues in the sentence. «Capybaras live in groups near rivers, lakes and swamps and are very good swimmers.» Rivers, lakes and swamps are all wet places, so a capybara must be an animal that lives in water and swims well. Always ask: what would fit here? Then test your idea by putting it into the sentence.
explain: Some words are just a clue to a bigger idea. A capybara is barrel-shaped, which means round and wide like a barrel, and has short brown fur. «Caimans rarely attack big grown capybaras, which are too big and alert.» This tells you that a grown capybara is large, so it is hard for a caiman to catch.
fact: A capybara is the world's largest rodent.
fact: A rodent is a group of animals that includes mice, rats and guinea pigs.
fact: Capybaras are big, calm, barrel-shaped animals with short brown fur.
fact: Capybaras live in groups near rivers, lakes and swamps in South America, and they are very good swimmers.
fact: Their eyes, ears and nostrils are on top of their head, so they can stay hidden in the water.
exercise: «The capybara is the world's largest rodent.» What is a capybara? | *A big rodent from South America | A type of boat for rivers | A kind of fish from the sea | A small bird that lives in trees
exercise: «Capybaras live in groups near rivers, lakes and swamps.» Where do capybaras live? | High up in dry mountains | Deep under the sea | *Near rivers, lakes and swamps | In hot desert sand
exercise: «The capybara's eyes, ears and nostrils are on top of its head.» Why might this help it? | It keeps its brown fur dry | It helps it dig deep holes | *It can stay hidden in water and still see and hear | It helps it fly over the trees
test: «Capybaras are very good swimmers.» What does this tell us? | *They are good at swimming | They cannot go into water at all | Swimmers are a kind of bird | They only eat fish
test: «Jaguars hunt caimans and capybaras (giant rodents).» What do the brackets tell you? | *A capybara is a very big rodent | A capybara is a type of jaguar | A jaguar is a kind of rodent | A caiman is a kind of rodent
exam: «The capybara is a barrel-shaped rodent, a close relative of the guinea pig.» Using the context, which animal is a capybara most like? | A jaguar | *A guinea pig | A shark | A bird
